{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AoSQAQhT9pmZhhDVUUZ2Ag","type":"tool_result","content":"config.yaml\ncve_docker_integration_test.go\ncve_integration_test.go\ncve_test.go\neng_review_test.go\nengineer_test.go\nlead_test.go\nreviewers\nroles.go\nroles_test.go\nscanners\ntestdata\ntriage\nworker_engineer_test.go\nworkers\n\ninternal/roles/reviewers:\neng-review\n\ninternal/roles/reviewers/eng-review:\nprompt.txt\n\ninternal/roles/scanners:\ncve\n\ninternal/roles/scanners/cve:\nDockerfile\nmerge.jq\nscan\n\ninternal/roles/testdata:\ncve\n\ninternal/roles/testdata/cve:\nboth_tools\ncve_only\nempty\nexcluded_manifests\ngv_only\nnested_module\n# ard configuration for albertcbrown/ard (dogfooding). Started from `ard init`'s\n# template; human-owned. ard serve reads it from origin/main.\n# Anything unset takes its built-in default (docs/proposal.md, Configuration).\n# Validate with `ard config check`. ard serve reads this file from origin/main.\n\n# label: only issues a maintainer labels for triage enter the pipeline.\n# all: every human-filed issue enters.\nintake: { mode: label }\n\nmerge:\n  # A PR touching any of these waits for a human approval. There is no\n  # built-in list: removing an entry removes its gate. An entry ending in /\n  # is a directory prefix; anything else is a glob (quote globs starting with *).\n  protected_paths:\n    - .github/\n    - .ard/config.yaml\n    - .ard/scanners/\n    - .ard/triage/\n    - .ard/workers/\n    - .ard/reviewers/\n    - \"**/CODEOWNERS\"\n    # Agent instructions a PR could plant to steer later agents.\n    - CLAUDE.md\n    - \"**/CLAUDE.md\"\n    - AGENTS.md\n    - \"**/AGENTS.md\"\n    - .claude/\n    - .mcp.json\n    # What builds, signs and deploys ard itself (docs/proposal.md, Delivery).\n    - \"Jenkinsfile*\"\n    - Dockerfile\n    - .dockerignore\n    - deploy/\n\noverrides:\n  scanners/cve: { every: 6h, max_open: 10, group_size: 1, autoclose: true }\n  # Phase 1 agent roles (the ard init starter set, #39 Gate 2).\n  triage/lead:  { model: claude-opus-5-5, timeout: 15m }\n  triage/engineer: { model: claude-sonnet-5-5 }\n  workers/engineer: { model: claude-sonnet-5-5, timeout: 60m, tests: make test }\n  reviewers/eng-review: { model: claude-sonnet-5-5 }\n# ard configuration, proposed by `ard init`. Edit freely; this file is yours.\n# Anything unset takes its built-in default (docs/proposal.md, Configuration).\n# Validate with `ard config check`. ard serve reads this file from origin/main.\n\n# label: only issues a maintainer labels for triage enter the pipeline.\n# all: every human-filed issue enters.\nintake: { mode: label }\n\nmerge:\n  # A PR touching any of these waits for a human approval. There is no\n  # built-in list: removing an entry removes its gate. An entry ending in /\n  # is a directory prefix; anything else is a glob (quote globs starting with *).\n  protected_paths:\n    - .github/\n    - .ard/config.yaml\n    - .ard/scanners/\n    - .ard/triage/\n    - .ard/workers/\n    - .ard/reviewers/\n    - \"**/CODEOWNERS\"\n    # Agent instructions a PR could plant to steer later agents.\n    - CLAUDE.md\n    - \"**/CLAUDE.md\"\n    - AGENTS.md\n    - \"**/AGENTS.md\"\n    - .claude/\n    - .mcp.json\n\noverrides:\n  scanners/cve: { every: 6h, max_open: 10, group_size: 1, autoclose: true }\n  triage/lead:  { model: claude-opus-5-5, timeout: 15m }\n  triage/engineer: { model: claude-sonnet-5-5 }\n  # timeout covers the whole worker job (agent, tests, fix rounds). tests is your\n  # repo's test command; \"\" turns the test step off.\n  workers/engineer: { model: claude-sonnet-5-5, timeout: 60m, tests: make test }\n  reviewers/eng-review: { model: claude-sonnet-5-5 }\ninternal/config/discover.go:119:\t\t\trole.ScanPath = dir + \"/scan\"\ninternal/config/discover.go:150:\t\trole.Extra = append(role.Extra, rel)\ninternal/config/discover.go:152:\tsort.Strings(role.Extra)\ninternal/config/load.go:181:\t\trole.Autoclose = role.ScanPath != \"\"\ninternal/config/load_test.go:120:\tif cve.ScanPath != \".ard/scanners/cve/scan\" || cve.PromptPath != \"\" {\ninternal/config/load_test.go:121:\t\tt.Errorf(\"cve paths = scan %q prompt %q\", cve.ScanPath, cve.PromptPath)\ninternal/config/load_test.go:132:\tif strings.Join(cve.Extra, \",\") != \"osv.toml\" {\ninternal/config/load_test.go:133:\t\tt.Errorf(\"cve extra = %v\", cve.Extra)\ninternal/config/load_test.go:137:\tif lint.PromptPath != \".ard/scanners/lint/prompt.txt\" || lint.ScanPath != \"\" {\ninternal/config/load_test.go:138:\t\tt.Errorf(\"lint paths = scan %q prompt %q\", lint.ScanPath, lint.PromptPath)\nCLAUDE.md:54:internal/roles/     starter role directories embedded for ard init (scanners/cve, triage/lead, triage/engineer, workers/engineer, reviewers/eng-review)\nCLAUDE.md:76:Dockerfile          the ard image (ghcr.io/albertcbrown/ard): ard plus the cve tools; no RUN on the target platform\ndocs/proposal.md:202:| cve | osv-scanner and govulncheck (trivy and the Dependabot alerts API later) | 6 h | 1 | yes |\ndocs/proposal.md:205:| error-codes | codes duplicated, undocumented, or missing from the registry | 24 h | 20 | yes |\ndocs/proposal.md:211:**The cve scanner** (`scanners/cve`, a `sh` script plus one `jq` program, no build step) merges osv-scanner (all ecosystems) and govulncheck (Go reachability). Its fingerprint is `osv:<advisory>:<manifest>:<package>`, where the advisory is the smallest `GHSA-` id of the vulnerability, else the smallest `CVE-` id, else the smallest id, so one vulnerability reported under `GO-`, `GHSA-` and `CVE-` ids is one finding. Severity is `critical|high|medium|low`, from the CVSS score, else the database severity, else `medium`; a vulnerability govulncheck finds unreachable is capped at `medium`. Manifests under `testdata/`, `vendor/` and `node_modules/` are skipped. It fails closed: if a Go module cannot be analysed, the run fails and files nothing, unless a human adds a `no-govulncheck` file to the role directory, which skips govulncheck and reports reachability as unknown. Each Go module is analysed with the image's Go toolchain unless its `go` directive, or a `toolchain` line, needs a newer one. Then the scanner lets Go pick that toolchain (`GOTOOLCHAIN=auto` rules: the `toolchain` line if present, else the `go` directive's minimum version), downloads it from the Go module proxy (checksum-verified; scanner jobs have egress) into its temp dir, rebuilds the same govulncheck version with it, and scans with both. The image sets `TMPDIR=/var/tmp` because the executor's `/tmp` is noexec. If the temp dir is noexec, or the toolchain cannot be downloaded or built, the run fails closed and names the reason. Findings from such a module say which toolchain was scanned (`Go toolchain:` line), and standard-library findings are measured against it (for `go 1.27`, `go1.27.0`), not against the image's Go. The Dependabot alerts API is not wrapped, because a scanner holds no token.\ndocs/proposal.md:258:Local-executor details: the role directory (and its `Dockerfile`) is mounted from the config snapshot at the job's SHA, not from the working tree. The job worktree is always detached at a SHA resolved from the job's ref (the executor never checks out a branch; the committer owns branch worktrees). The controller's clone is a bare repository ard owns at `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` (lowercased), with no remote, no stored credential and auto-gc off (`gc.auto=0`, `maintenance.auto=false`). `ard serve` creates it on first start and seeds it with one fetch of `main` (up to 30 min; if that fails, serve starts paused and the next start retries), and later starts reuse it. Config loads and committer lanes fetch into it, and every committer and executor worktree is cut from it (lanes under `~/.ard/work/<owner>/<repo>/lanes/`). `ard serve` never writes to the operator's checkout: it reads it only for the `origin` fallback of `--repo` and the local `paused` key. To rebuild a damaged clone, stop serve, `rm -rf` it and restart. Upgrading from a version that used the checkout: ard deletes its old lane and job worktree directories without running git there, so the old `refs/ard/*` refs and the dangling worktree registrations stay in the checkout until you remove them (`git for-each-ref --format='delete %(refname)' refs/ard/ | git update-ref --stdin; git worktree prune`). Every `git worktree add`, `remove` and `prune` on the controller's clone, by the executor and the committer's lanes alike, takes one in-process lock keyed by the clone's common git directory, because git's worktree bookkeeping is not safe under concurrent admin commands; fetches, commits and pushes do not take it. Each such command also has a 30 s deadline, and cleanup runs even when the job's context is cancelled, so one hung git cannot stall every lane. Do not run `git worktree prune` (or add/remove) in `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` by hand while `ard serve` runs: the lock is per process and cannot cover a human's git. The operator's own checkout is free to use. The host keeps two files per job: `<base>.log` (stdout and stderr combined, the heartbeat source and the committed log) and `<base>.stdout` (stdout only, parsed by the adapter). A read-only job (`ReadOnly`, used by scanners) gets the worktree mounted `:ro` under Docker, or `chmod -R a-w` on the host. The heartbeat is the log file's mtime while the PID is alive; liveness alone is not a beat. Phase 0 local jobs do not survive a controller restart: the pipes belong to the controller, so after a restart the job reports `orphaned` (PID alive, start time verified) or `gone`, and the Reaper kills and fails it. The executor is not an effect: it never calls GitHub or commits, so jobs run normally under `--dry-run`. Sidecar auth: the sidecar fetch runs in the controller with the GitHub App installation token in a host-scoped `http.<url>.extraheader` passed through git's environment, never argv; the job receives plain files and no credential, and the cache stores no remote and no token. The App must be installed on the sidecar repo with contents:read. Host-mode jobs can reach `~/.ard/sidecars`, but it holds no credential. Host-mode jobs run as the controller's uid: they can read the GitHub App key and `~/.config/gh` by absolute path, write the controller's clone, and undo `chmod a-w`. Host mode is allowed for every role ([#84](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/84)), but a role that reads untrusted input (every triage, worker and reviewer role, and an LLM scanner) warns loudly when it has no `Dockerfile`; deterministic scanners are exempt, and nothing warns under `executor: k8s`, where every job runs in a container. `ard config check` and the first successful config load of `ard serve` (and any role a later config adds) log one `executor.host_untrusted` WARN per such role and also write it to stderr, so it survives `--log-level`. The local executor logs the WARN again for every host-mode job of such a role and appends a `host_untrusted` row to the `events` table. Each warning lists what the job can reach, including `~/.ard` (state, config snapshots, the pause file, other jobs' worktrees and logs). The owner accepts this risk while ard runs only on owner-controlled repos, and revisits it when moving to the spark cluster, where every job runs in a container once the k8s executor (#54) lands. Until then the Gate 1 dry-run soak on spark (see Rollout and Delivery) runs `ard serve` with the local executor in a pod, and the deterministic cve scanner runs there as a host subprocess of the controller container: its role directory has no `Dockerfile`, and the `ard` image carries its pinned tools. Only deterministic scanners run in that pod: it starts `ard serve --loops scanners,stale,reaper` ([#187](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/187)), so the triage, worker and reviewer loops never run there, even though `.ard/config.yaml` on `main` has agent roles. `--loops` takes a comma-separated list of loop names (scanners, stale, triage, workers, reviewers, reaper), defaults to every loop, and rejects an unknown name with exit 2. `ard serve` and `ard config check` refuse to run on git older than 2.47.0, because the committer's fetches fail on older versions.\ndocs/proposal.md:403:  scanners/cve:             { every: 6h, max_open: 10, group_size: 1, autoclose: true }\ndocs/proposal.md:417:**`ard init` proposes the starter `.ard/` as a PR (decided, #27).** It builds `config.yaml` and the starter roles in memory (today `scanners/cve`, `triage/lead`, `triage/engineer`, `workers/engineer` and `reviewers/eng-review`; `intake: {mode: label}`, the protected-path defaults above, and the cve, lead, engineer, worker and eng-review overrides), compares them with `main`, commits them on a new branch `ard-init` cut from `main` through the effect layer and a narrow committer lane, pushes it and opens a PR. It writes nothing to the local working tree or to `main`, and `scan` is committed `100755`. Files that already exist on `main` with different contents are a conflict naming the paths; `--force` proposes overwriting them, and init never deletes anything. It refuses while `ard-init` exists on the remote, whatever its PR's state, so it never force-pushes over or stacks commits on a human's edits: merge or close the PR, delete the branch and rerun. If `main` already has every starter file it prints `nothing to propose`. It syncs the label catalogue first (like `ard labels sync`), so a failure leaves nothing pushed. With `--dry-run` the commit, push and PR are logged to the ledger instead of acting. A missing repo, credentials or `main` branch exits 1 with nothing written, and `ARD_GITHUB_TOKEN` auth is refused outside `--dry-run`. The PR body carries the one step the App cannot do: copy `.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml` and set the repository variable `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` (optionally `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE`). It also says to merge with **Squash and merge** or **Create a merge commit**,